{
  "Description": "The SAME coil (circular wire, mean radius 10, wire radius 2, 3 turns, pitch 8) is built five times from five separate but identical sketches. A helical sweep is a shear of a torus and shear preserves volume, so the exact volume is A * 2*pi*R * turns = (pi*2^2) * 2*pi*10 * 3 = 2368.705056. (2368.6667987045694 with precision=bounds*1e-4)",
  "Expected": "All five volumes identical and positive. At the default precision of 1e-4 inspect.properties reports about 2368.66, which is the volume-integration accuracy and not a geometry error - measured with precision 1e-6 the same body gives 2368.705052, i.e. correct to 9 digits.",
  "Status": "FIXED. Re-checked 2026-08-10: all five volumes identical and positive, and 8 further runs in separate batches gave +2368.6613 every time, stable to the 11th digit. Keep as a regression guard.",
  "Actual (before the fix)": "The orientation of the resulting shell was not stable - identical input produced a positive or a negative volume. Observed pattern in two independent experiments: coil1, coil2, coil3, coil5 negative and coil4 POSITIVE. The positive instance also differed in the last digits, so it was a different result and not only a flipped sign. This was the root cause behind most of the other Helical*.json cases: an inverted shell fed into solid.boolean either threw or silently swapped intersect and difference.",
  "Resolved": "The deviation from the exact volume at the default precision was NOT a geometry error - it is the reduced volume-integration accuracy. Measured with the new inspect.properties 'precision' parameter: 1e-4 gives 2368.661272 (rel. err 1.9e-5), 1e-5 gives 2368.704593 (2.0e-7), 1e-6 gives 2368.705052 (1.9e-9). Clean convergence, so the helical face is fine.",
  "CaseStatus": "Ok",
